// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`project --help > project 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project [options] [command]

项目管理 pjm: projects and work items (scope pcp:read:pjm:project)

Options:
  -h, --help                    display help for command

Commands:
  list [options]                list projects
  get [options] <project>       show one project
  create [options]              create a project — PERMANENT: there is no delete and no archive
  update [options] <project>    patch a project — only the fields you pass are sent
  progress [options] <project>  work-item counts for a project (open / in progress / done)
  work-item                     work items (scopes pcp:read:pjm:workitem / pcp:write:pjm:workitem)
  sprint                        迭代 sprints: plan and update them (scopes pcp:read:pjm:sprint /
                                pcp:write:pjm:sprint)
  version                       发布 releases of a project (scopes pcp:read:pjm:release /
                                pcp:write:pjm:release)
  board                         看板 boards: list boards, entries and swimlanes (scopes
                                pcp:read:pjm:board / pcp:read:pjm:boardentry /
                                pcp:read:pjm:boardswimlane)
  member                        项目成员 members of a project (scopes pcp:read:pjm:project /
                                pcp:write:pjm:project)
  meta                          ids you need before writing: every pjm lookup is scoped to one
                                project
  help [command]                display help for command
"
`;

exports[`project --help > project create (permanent, and the one flag that can never be changed) 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project create [options]

create a project — PERMANENT: there is no delete and no archive

Options:
  --name <text>              project name
  --identifier <KEY>         short uppercase key, unique in the organisation — it prefixes every
                             work-item id
  --type <type>              one of scrum | kanban | waterfall | hybrid
  --description <text>       description
  --visibility <visibility>  private (default) | public — CANNOT be changed later
  --assignee <name|id>       负责人; need not be a member, and is not added as one
  --start-at <when>          unix seconds or a date like 2026-01-31 — stored verbatim, not snapped
                             to a day
  --end-at <when>            unix seconds or a date like 2026-01-31 — stored verbatim, not snapped
                             to a day
  --member <name|id>         initial member, repeatable: user name or id
  --process-id <id>          project process template; omitted, --type picks the matching system
                             one (\`pingcode api GET /v1/pjm/processes\`)
  -h, --help                 display help for command

This is IRREVERSIBLE. The API has no DELETE for a project, and \`is_archived\` is
read-only (a patch containing it is accepted and silently dropped), so a project
created by mistake stays in every listing forever. There is no --dry-run substitute
for reading this paragraph, but \`--dry-run\` does show the exact body first.
--identifier must be uppercase with no spaces and reasonably short; anything else is
rejected as a bad format rather than truncated. It is the only required field that
can still be changed afterwards, with \`project update --identifier\`.
--visibility can only be set here: patching it later does nothing.
Unlike a sprint or a release, --start-at / --end-at are stored EXACTLY as given —
the server does not round them to the start and end of the day.

"
`;

exports[`project --help > project member (the deliberate absence of \`remove\` is in the group help) 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project member [options] [command]

项目成员 members of a project (scopes pcp:read:pjm:project / pcp:write:pjm:project)

Options:
  -h, --help            display help for command

Commands:
  list [options]        list the members of a project (user memberships are the only valid
                        --assignee candidates)
  get [options] <user>  show one membership, addressed by the user it belongs to
  add [options]         add a user or a team to a project
  help [command]        display help for command

A membership is addressed by the USER id, not by a separate membership id — that is
what the API returns as the row id, so \`member get <user>\` is the same reference
\`member add --user\` took.
There is no \`member remove\` leaf: the endpoint exists, and the generic layer reaches
it — pingcode api DELETE /v1/pjm/projects/<project>/members/<user> --yes
It is not a refined leaf because a membership is trivially recreatable with
\`member add\`, and this command group is capped at the endpoints it was scoped for.
Note the project's 负责人 (owner) is unrelated to membership: it can be someone
who is not a member, and setting it does not add them.
Work-item assignees are different: they MUST be project members — the CLI blocks
assignment of non-members, because a non-member cannot see the card.

"
`;

exports[`project --help > project meta 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project meta [options] [command]

ids you need before writing: every pjm lookup is scoped to one project

Options:
  -h, --help                display help for command

Commands:
  types [options]           work-item types of a project (values for --type / type_id)
  states [options]          work-item states of one (project, type) pair — both are required
  priorities [options]      work-item priorities of a project
  sprints [options]         sprints of a project (scrum/hybrid only)
  relation-types [options]  link types for \`work-item link add\` (organisation-wide, 9 system rows)
  tags [options]            work-item tags — the only tag enumerator, and it is ORGANISATION-wide
  help [command]            display help for command
"
`;

exports[`project --help > project meta tags (the org-wide vocabulary warning) 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project meta tags [options]

work-item tags — the only tag enumerator, and it is ORGANISATION-wide

Options:
  --project <name|id>  project name or id (required by the API, but see below)
  --name <text>        name SUBSTRING, case-insensitive — a search, not an exact match
  -h, --help           display help for command

Read this before using an id from here.
--project is REQUIRED by the endpoint and then IGNORED by it: three different
projects returned byte-identical lists (verified live 2026-08-04). So what you get
is every tag in the organisation, not this project's.
Tags are nevertheless project-scoped where it counts: \`work-item tag add\` refuses a
tag belonging to another project with "'tag'资源不存在", which reads as though the
tag did not exist. It does — it is simply not this project's. There is no endpoint
that lists only a given project's tags, so the reliable way to find a usable id is
to read the tags[] of a work item in that project (\`work-item get\`).
Names are not unique either: several projects each define their own 后端 / 前端.
This is also why \`pingcode resolve\` has no work-item-tag kind — a cached resolver
would hand back ids the write refuses.

"
`;

