exports[`project --help > project version update (--operate-at needs --stage-id) 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project version update [options] <version>

patch a release — only the fields you pass are sent, and arrays replace

Arguments:
  version               release 发布 name or id

Options:
  --project <name|id>   project name or id (the API does not check this one)
  --name <text>         new name
  --start <date>        new start — a date (2026-08-31) or a 10-digit unix seconds value, stored at
                        00:00:00 local
  --end <date>          new end — a date (2026-08-31) or a 10-digit unix seconds value, stored at
                        23:59:59 local
  --assignee <name|id>  new owner
  --stage-id <id>       move to this stage; needs --operate-at unless the release has been in it
                        before
  --operate-at <date>   when the release reached --stage-id — a date (2026-08-31) or a 10-digit
                        unix seconds value, read at 00:00:00 local like --start. Must fall inside
                        the release window, and is only valid WITH --stage-id: alone the API
                        accepts it, echoes the old value and stores nothing
  --category-id <id>    repeatable; replaces the whole set
  -h, --help            display help for command
"
`;

exports[`project --help > project work-item 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project work-item [options] [command]

work items (scopes pcp:read:pjm:workitem / pcp:write:pjm:workitem)

Options:
  -h, --help                        display help for command

Commands:
  list [options]                    list work items of a project (some filters switch to the search
                                    endpoint)
  get [options] <work-item>         show one work item
  create [options]                  create a work item (ids are project-scoped: run \`pingcode
                                    project meta\` first)
  update [options] <work-item>      patch a work item — only the fields you pass are sent, and they
                                    replace
  transition [options] <work-item>  move a work item to another state (workflow-validated by the
                                    server)
  bulk-update [options]             set ONE property on many work items in a single call
  delete [options] <work-item>      delete a work item — recoverable from the recycle bin in the
                                    web UI
  link                              typed links between two WORK ITEMS (blocks, duplicates,
                                    relates, …)
  tag                               标签 tags on a work item (add / get / remove)
  history                           流转记录 the STATE history of a work item (read-only)
  relation                          关联 cross-kind links of a work item (principal_type=work_item)
  comment                           评论 comments on a work item (principal_type=work_item)
  attachment                        附件 attachments of a work item (principal_type=work_item)
  activity                          活动记录 audit trail of a work item (principal_type=work_item)
  help [command]                    display help for command
"
`;

exports[`project --help > project work-item bulk-update (the widest new flag set, and the ignored properties) 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project work-item bulk-update [options]

set ONE property on many work items in a single call

Options:
  --id <ref>            work item to change, repeatable: id, short_id, identifier or URL
  --project <name|id>   scope for resolving --state / --priority by NAME
  --type <name|id>      work-item type, needed with --state <name>
  --assignee <name|id>  new assignee — must be a project member (requires --project)
  --assignee-id <id>    new assignee, given as an id — must be a project member (requires
                        --project)
  --priority <name>     new priority (needs --project)
  --priority-id <id>    new priority, given as an id
  --title <text>        new title — the same title for every work item
  --description <text>  new description — the same one for every work item
  --property <name>     any other property_name, for a property this CLI has no flag for
  --value <json>        the value for --property: JSON if it parses, otherwise the raw string
  --state <name>        new state, resolved by name (requires --type)
  --state-id <id>       new state, given as an id (no lookup, no --type needed)
  -h, --help            display help for command

The endpoint takes ONE property_name and ONE value, so exactly one of --assignee /
--assignee-id / --state / --state-id / --priority / --priority-id / --title /
--description / --property must be given. Two properties need two calls.
Verified to work upstream: assignee_id, state_id, priority_id, title, description.
Verified ACCEPTED AND SILENTLY IGNORED (HTTP 200, "updated 0"): sprint_id and
version_ids — so a bulk move into a sprint or onto a release is NOT possible here,
however valid the id. Do it ONE AT A TIME with \`work-item update --sprint <s>\` /
\`work-item update --release <r>\`, which patch the single-item endpoint and do work.
Also ignored here: type_id, tag_ids, participant_ids, properties, bug_type_id,
entry_id, swimlane_id, phase_id. parent_id and board_id are refused outright.
--property is the escape hatch for anything not listed; it is not validated, and a
property the server ignores still answers 200, so always read the "updated" count.
Other live facts worth knowing before scripting this:
  - it is BEST EFFORT, not atomic: an unknown id is skipped silently and the rest
    still land (the sprint/release bulk creates are atomic — do not generalise);
  - the change appears in NO activity record, unlike the single-item update, so it
    is invisible to an audit;
  - the ids may span projects, which is why --state / --priority by name need
    --project: their ids are project-scoped and one batch may not share a project;
  - each --id costs one read, because the endpoint accepts real ids only.

"
`;

exports[`project --help > project work-item delete (the first refined DELETE in pjm) 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project work-item delete [options] <work-item>

delete a work item — recoverable from the recycle bin in the web UI

Arguments:
  work-item   id, short_id, identifier such as SCR-5, or a pasted URL

Options:
  --yes       confirm: the item leaves every list, sprint and board immediately
  -h, --help  display help for command
"
`;

exports[`project --help > project work-item history (state changes only) 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project work-item history [options] [command]

流转记录 the STATE history of a work item (read-only)

Options:
  -h, --help                              display help for command

Commands:
  list [options] <work-item>              list the state changes of a work item
  get [options] <work-item> <history-id>  show one state change
  help [command]                          display help for command

State changes only. A title, assignee or sprint change is not here — that is the
\`activity\` subgroup, which is the free-form audit feed. Every work item has one row
from creation, with FROM shown as (new).
A bulk update (\`work-item bulk-update\`) appears in NEITHER feed, verified live: it
changes the record without recording that it did.

"
`;

exports[`project --help > project work-item link (link vs relation is in the group help) 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project work-item link [options] [command]

typed links between two WORK ITEMS (blocks, duplicates, relates, …)

Options:
  -h, --help                              display help for command

Commands:
  list [options] <work-item>              list the work items linked to this one
  get [options] <work-item> <link-id>     show one link — the work item in the path is checked,
                                          unlike a release
  add [options] <work-item>               link this work item to another one, with a type
  delete [options] <work-item> <link-id>  remove a link — both directions go at once
  help [command]                          display help for command

Use \`link\` for work item ↔ work item and \`relation\` for work item ↔ anything else:
the cross-kind /v1/relations family refuses two work items, and this one accepts
nothing but work items. Only this family has a type.
List the types with \`pingcode project meta relation-types\`. --relation takes the
localized name (关联), the stable category slug (relate) or the id; the slug is the
safest, because the ids differ per tenant.
The server keeps the INVERSE edge in step: adding "block" here adds "blocked_by"
there, and deleting either side removes both. The two sides have DIFFERENT link
ids, so delete the one \`link list\` printed for the work item you are on.
Links may cross projects, and a work item can be linked to itself (the API allows
it; nothing here stops you).

"
`;

exports[`project --help > project work-item list (the two transports and their different filters) 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project work-item list [options]

list work items of a project (some filters switch to the search endpoint)

Options:
  --project <name|id>            project name or id
  --type <name|id>               work-item type
  --assignee <name|id>           assignee: display name, username, email or id — must be a project
                                 member
  --sprint <name|id>             sprint (scrum/hybrid projects only)
  --parent <ref>                 only children of this work item: id, short_id, identifier or URL
  --keywords <text>              fuzzy search over title and description
  --identifier <text>            work-item identifier such as SCR-5 (REST list only)
  --priority <name|id>           priority
  --board <name|id>              board
  --entry <name|id>              board entry (column)
  --swimlane <name|id>           swimlane
  --phase <name|id>              phase
  --release <name|id>            release / version
  --tag <name|id>                tag
  --bug-type <id>                bug type id (REST list only, pass-through)
  --created-by <name|id>         creator: display name, username, email or id
  --participant <name|id>        participant: display name, username, email or id
  --unassigned                   (search) only work items with no assignee
  --title-contains <text>        (search) substring of the title
  --description-contains <text>  (search) substring of the description
  --start-after <date>           (search) start on or after this DATE
  --start-before <date>          (search) start on or before this DATE
  --end-after <date>             (search) end on or after this DATE
  --end-before <date>            (search) end on or before this DATE
  --completed-after <date>       (search) completed on or after this DATE
  --completed-before <date>      (search) completed on or before this DATE
  --story-points <n>             (search) story points (exact match)
  --created-after <date>         (search) created on or after this DATE (00:00 local)
  --created-before <date>        (search) created on or before this DATE (23:59 local)
  --updated-after <date>         (search) updated on or after this DATE
  --updated-before <date>        (search) updated on or before this DATE
  --page <n>                     page index, 0-based (default: "0")
  --page-size <n>                rows per page, 1-100 (default: "30")
  --all                          walk every page (best effort: the API guarantees no ordering)
  --limit <n>                    stop after this many rows with --all (default: "500")
  --state <name>                 filter by state, resolved by name (resolves across all types if
                                 --type is omitted)
  --state-id <id>                filter by state, given as an id (no lookup, no --type needed)
  -h, --help                     display help for command

Flags marked (search) can only be expressed by POST /v1/pjm/work_items/search, so
passing any of them switches this command to that endpoint. Paging, --all and the
reported total behave identically on both transports (verified live 2026-08-04), so
the switch is invisible except for which filters are available.
The two endpoints do filter on different things. Search cannot filter by
identifier, short_id or bug type at all; the simple list cannot filter by date,
title/description text, "unassigned" or story points. Everything else works in both.
Search-only flags: --title-contains, --description-contains, --unassigned,
--start-after/before, --end-after/before, --completed-after/before, --story-points,
--created-after/before, --updated-after/before.
REST-only flags: --identifier, --bug-type.

"
`;

exports[`project --help > project work-item tag (the missing list is in the group help) 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project work-item tag [options] [command]

标签 tags on a work item (add / get / remove)

Options:
  -h, --help                             display help for command

Commands:
  add [options] <work-item>              add a tag to a work item
  get [options] <work-item> <tag-id>     show one tag of a work item (there is no list — see above)
  delete [options] <work-item> <tag-id>  remove a tag from a work item (the tag itself survives)
  help [command]                         display help for command

There is no \`tag list\`: the API has no collection GET here (only add, get-one and
remove), so read the tags[] field of \`work-item get\` instead. Enumerate the
vocabulary with \`pingcode project meta tags --project <p>\`.
Tags are PROJECT-scoped on the write side but the vocabulary list is org-wide, so
most ids it prints belong to other projects and are refused here with
"'tag'资源不存在". If that happens, the tag exists — it just is not this project's.
Tag names are not unique either, so --tag <name> can be ambiguous; --tag-id always
works.
A second remove is safe HERE — this leaf reads the tag first, so it fails cleanly with
"工作项不包含此标签". The raw endpoint answers HTTP 500 to a repeat DELETE, so reach it
through \`pingcode api DELETE\` only if you are ready for that.

"
`;

exports[`project --help > project work-item transition (--type is a lookup aid, not a patched field) 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project work-item transition [options] <work-item>

move a work item to another state (workflow-validated by the server)

Arguments:
  work-item         id, short_id, identifier such as SCR-5, or a pasted URL

Options:
  --type <name|id>  work-item type — resolves --state <name> and lists candidate states on
                    rejection; does NOT modify the type (the API has no type_id in PATCH)
  --state <name>    target state, resolved by name (requires --type)
  --state-id <id>   target state, given as an id (no lookup, no --type needed)
  -h, --help        display help for command
"
`;

exports[`project --help > project work-item update (the widest flag set) 1`] = `
"Usage: pingcode project work-item update [options] <work-item>

patch a work item — only the fields you pass are sent, and they replace

Arguments:
  work-item                 id, short_id, identifier such as SCR-5, or a pasted URL

Options:
  --title <text>            new title
  --description <text>      new description (replaces the old one)
  --type <name|id>          work-item type — resolves --state <name> and lists candidate states on
                            rejection; does NOT modify the type (the API has no type_id in PATCH)
  --assignee <name|id>      new assignee — must be a project member (the Open API cannot clear it —
                            use the Web UI to unassign)
  --priority <name|id>      new priority
  --parent <ref>            new parent work item
  --sprint <name|id>        move into this sprint (scrum/hybrid projects only)
  --clear-sprint            remove the work item from its current sprint
  --release <name|id>       发布/release to put this item on, repeatable — replaces the whole list
                            (--version is the CLI's own flag)
  --start-at <when>         unix seconds or a date like 2026-01-31
  --end-at <when>           unix seconds or a date like 2026-01-31
  --story-points <n>        story points
  --estimated-workload <n>  estimated workload in hours
  --remaining-workload <n>  remaining workload in hours
  --board <name|id>         看板 board — list with \`project board list\`
  --entry <name|id>         看板栏 board entry — list with \`project board entries list\`
  --swimlane <name|id>      泳道 swimlane — list with \`project board swimlanes list\`
  --state <name>            new state, resolved by name (requires --type)
  --state-id <id>           new state, given as an id (no lookup, no --type needed)
  -h, --help                display help for command
"
`;
